{%- comment -%}
  SHUDHI — Soap Plate

  The homepage Signature Product band's left column: a handcrafted soap bar under
  soft upper-left studio light, inside an asymmetric floating botanical ecosystem.
  Replaces the "Our collection.webp" packshot that used to sit here.

  Called from sections/sh-signature-product.liquid. Styled in shudhi-home.css §25.
  Animated by assets/sh-soap-plate.js — but only as an enhancement; see (3).

  Parameters
    uid      REQUIRED. Pass section.id. See (1).
    animate  emits [data-reveal] so the shared observer drives the entrance
    motion   false emits [data-plate-still], which §25 uses to stop every loop
    cursor   false omits every [data-plate-depth], so the JS finds nothing to bind

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SEVEN THINGS HERE ARE LOAD-BEARING
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. EVERY id IS NAMESPACED FROM `uid`, and uid is required rather than optional.
     SVG ids are document-global. This section ships a preset so it can be added
     twice, and sections/sh-our-story.liquid renders a seal on the same page. Two
     plates sharing ids would BOTH resolve every url(#…) against the FIRST one's
     <defs> — and because the art is identical, it would look perfectly correct
     until someone tuned them differently, at which point one plate would silently
     change colour. Note the `default` runs BEFORE the append: `'sh-plate-' |
     append: nil` returns the non-blank string "sh-plate-", so a `default` chained
     after the append could never fire. Same trap snippets/sh-gen-seal.liquid:47
     documents.

  2. EVERY url(#…) LIVES IN THIS FILE, never in §25. A stylesheet cannot see
     section.id, so a gradient referenced from CSS cannot be namespaced, and
     would reintroduce (1) by the back door. Paint that varies goes in a
     presentation attribute here; paint that is constant can go in CSS.

  3. THE RESTING STATE IS THE FINISHED STATE. Nothing here starts hidden. The
     entrance is opted INTO by a class the JS adds (.is-armed), never a
     pre-state the JS has to clear. The photo this replaces got that backwards —
     it rested at `clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0)`, so any failure between parse
     and the observer firing left a permanently empty column. JS off, a 404 on
     sh-soap-plate.js, an exception earlier in boot: all of them land here on a
     complete, visible illustration.

     The sheen obeys this too. It is parked at x -280…-69, entirely clear of the
     bar, so with no animation running there is no stray bright band sitting on
     the soap.

  4. FOUR NESTING LEVELS, ONE JOB EACH — because an element cannot hold two
     transforms and the second one silently wins:

         .sh-plate__stage    translateY .......... entrance lift
         .sh-plate__depth    translate3d ......... cursor parallax (JS writes this)
         .sh-plate__layer    opacity ............. staggered reveal
         .sh-plate__leaf-*   rotate/translate .... the ambient loop

     Where a static placement transform is also needed, it goes on a WRAPPER
     around the animated element, never on it. The neem is the worked example.

  5. NO <filter>. Not one. feGaussianBlur and feDropShadow force a per-frame CPU
     raster, and this subtree animates continuously — six loops, always running
     while on screen. Every soft edge here is a multi-stop alpha ramp inside a
     gradient instead: same look, composited, free. Adding a filter or a
     mix-blend-mode anywhere inside also defeats the single flattened layer the
     ~118 elements currently collapse into. Do not add one.

  6. THE MOBILE CROP CONTRACT: keep every coordinate inside y ∈ [130, 820].
     preserveAspectRatio is `slice`, so the box is COVERED, not fitted. The worst
     case is the square crop at ≤989px: a 760×950 viewBox covering a square loses
     (950 − 760) / 2 = 95 units off the top AND bottom, leaving y ∈ [95, 855]
     visible. The band above is that, plus 35 units of margin. A coordinate
     outside it vanishes on phones and on nothing else, which is exactly the kind
     of bug that ships. Horizontal is never cropped — the viewBox is the narrower
     ratio — so x is unconstrained.

  7. THE SEAL'S CORNER IS RESERVED. §25 pins .sh-gen at bottom:10%/left:1.5%,
     landing on viewBox x 11–114, y 752–855. Nothing in the artwork may enter it.
     The tulsi is the only cluster that comes close and is held at x ≥ 208.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LAYOUT — measured, not eyeballed. Numbers are viewBox units.
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    soap bar     x 154–596, y 430–604 (58% of frame width) — the hero
    neem         x 546–652, y 311–466, tucked BEHIND the bar's top-right
    herb sprig   x 152–236, y 268–436 — upper left, faintest thing in the frame
    bud          x 314–354, y 240–366 — off-centre left, breaks the top void
    droplet      x 646–674, y 486–528 — right edge, below the neem
    tulsi        x 208–292, y 615–806 — FOREGROUND, crosses the bar's lower left
    low herb     x 556–668, y 668–760 — foreground counterweight, bottom right
    seal (HTML)  x  11–114, y 752–855 — see (7)

  The bar's centre sits at 49% width / 54% height. Deliberately not the middle:
  the copy column to the right is top-weighted, so the illustration's mass sits
  low and left of centre to balance it across the gutter rather than mirror it.

  Element budget: ~118 elements including <defs>. That is roughly 4× the largest
  SVG this theme otherwise ships. The nine neem leaflets are <use> references to
  one <path> for exactly that reason — do not inline them. If this ever needs to
  pass 200, it wants to be a raster instead.

  Light: a single key from the upper left, everywhere, no exceptions. The top face
  is brightest, the front face mid, the right end darkest because it is turned
  away. The bevel highlight runs the back and left edges only. Background
  botanicals are painted lighter and flatter than foreground ones — aerial
  perspective, and it is what separates the depth layers when parallax is off.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign plate_uid = uid | default: 'plate' -%}
{%- assign gid = 'sh-plate-' | append: plate_uid -%}

{%- comment -%} data-plate-reveal, NOT data-reveal, and the difference is the
    whole of (3). [data-reveal] would hand the entrance to the shared observer in
    shudhi-home.js — which works, but couples the plate to a SECOND script: with
    this file's JS present and shudhi-home.js failing, the plate would arm itself
    and then wait forever for an .is-in that never arrives, i.e. an invisible
    column again, by a new route. Owning the entrance end to end means exactly one
    script can fail, and if it does, nothing was ever hidden. {%- endcomment -%}
<div class="sh-plate"
     {% if animate %}data-plate-reveal{% endif %}{% unless motion %} data-plate-still{% endunless %}>

  <svg class="sh-plate__svg"
       viewBox="0 0 760 950"
       preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice"
       aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">

    <defs>
      {%- comment -%} ---- Soap material ---------------------------------- {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} The top face carries the key. Three stops, not two: the
          middle one is what stops a 460-unit-wide face reading as flat vinyl. {%- endcomment -%}
      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-top" x1="0.12" y1="0" x2="0.88" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#f4e9d3"/>
        <stop offset="0.46" stop-color="#e0cca6"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#c8ac80"/>
      </linearGradient>

      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-front" x1="0.1" y1="0" x2="0.7" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#cdb289"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#a08657"/>
      </linearGradient>

      {%- comment -%} Turned furthest from the key, so it is the darkest plane in
          the whole illustration. Everything else is lighter than this. {%- endcomment -%}
      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-end" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#a68a58"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#896f46"/>
      </linearGradient>

      {%- comment -%} Ambient occlusion — the dark crease where the bar meets its
          own shadow. Tight and weak. This is the one that sells contact; the
          broad shadow below only sells distance. {%- endcomment -%}
      <radialGradient id="{{ gid }}-ao" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#6b5836" stop-opacity="0.32"/>
        <stop offset="0.55" stop-color="#6b5836" stop-opacity="0.15"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#6b5836" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>

      {%- comment -%} Four stops, because a two-stop radial has a visible hard
          shoulder at this size and that shoulder is exactly what a drop-shadow
          filter would have been hired to hide. {%- endcomment -%}
      <radialGradient id="{{ gid }}-shadow" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#7a5d2e" stop-opacity="0.24"/>
        <stop offset="0.40" stop-color="#7a5d2e" stop-opacity="0.14"/>
        <stop offset="0.72" stop-color="#7a5d2e" stop-opacity="0.05"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#7a5d2e" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>

      {%- comment -%} The atmosphere behind everything. Its outer stop is the
          section's own cream AT OPACITY 0, so the glow dissolves into whatever
          the merchant sets `bg` to instead of floating as a lighter card on top
          of it. That one value is why the schema needs no colour setting. {%- endcomment -%}
      <radialGradient id="{{ gid }}-glow" cx="0.5" cy="0.5" r="0.5">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#fdf7ec" stop-opacity="0.9"/>
        <stop offset="0.52" stop-color="#f5ead6" stop-opacity="0.45"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#f6ede1" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>

      {%- comment -%} Transparent at BOTH ends. A sheen with an opaque edge reads
          as a moving rectangle, not as light. {%- endcomment -%}
      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-sheen" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="0">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0"/>
        <stop offset="0.45" stop-color="#fff8e8" stop-opacity="0.34"/>
        <stop offset="0.55" stop-color="#fff8e8" stop-opacity="0.34"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </linearGradient>

      {%- comment -%} ---- Botanical material ------------------------------ {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Two greens, and the difference between them IS the depth
          cue. -leaf-b is lighter, warmer and lower-contrast: haze. It is what
          keeps the neem sitting behind the soap when parallax is off, on touch,
          and under reduced motion — i.e. most of the time. {%- endcomment -%}
      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-leaf-a" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0.85" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0" stop-color="#7c8d5e"/>
        <stop offset="1" stop-color="#4d6036"/>
      </linearGradient>

      <linearGradient id="{{ gid }}-leaf-b" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0.85" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0" stop-color="#a9b48d"/>
        <stop offset="1" stop-color="#8a9a6b"/>
      </linearGradient>

      <radialGradient id="{{ gid }}-drop" cx="0.36" cy="0.30" r="0.72">
        <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0.92"/>
        <stop offset="0.42" stop-color="#eef3e6" stop-opacity="0.46"/>
        <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#b9c39c" stop-opacity="0.30"/>
      </radialGradient>

      {%- comment -%} ---- Reusable geometry -------------------------------- {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} One neem leaflet, drawn once, used nine times. This single
          decision is most of the element budget above. {%- endcomment -%}
      <path id="{{ gid }}-leaflet" d="M0 0 C8 -4.4 21 -3.6 30 0 C21 3.6 8 4.4 0 0 Z"/>

      {%- comment -%} One tulsi leaf: blade plus midrib. A <g>, not a <path>,
          because the midrib is a stroke and the blade is a fill. {%- endcomment -%}
      <g id="{{ gid }}-tulsi">
        <path class="sh-plate__blade" d="M0 0 C11 -19 35 -27 56 -21 C46 3 21 11 0 0 Z"/>
        <path class="sh-plate__midrib" d="M2 -1 C18 -7 38 -13 54 -20"/>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} Clips the sheen to the top face. Byte-identical to the top
          face path below — if one is edited the other must be too, or the light
          runs off the edge of the solid. {%- endcomment -%}
      <clipPath id="{{ gid }}-topclip">
        <path d="M188 540 C300 534 460 536 596 540 C585 505 573 470 562 436
                 C450 430 290 431 154 436 C165 470 177 505 188 540 Z"/>
      </clipPath>
    </defs>

    <g class="sh-plate__stage">

      {%- comment -%} ================= L0 · ground ========================= {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Depth 0.1: it is the atmosphere, and atmosphere does not
          slide. Anything above ~0.15 here reads as the background peeling. {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="0.1"{% endif %}>
        <g class="sh-plate__layer">
          <ellipse cx="378" cy="512" rx="372" ry="312" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-glow)"/>
          {%- comment -%} Unrotated, unlike the bar above it. A shadow lies on the
              ground plane; tilting it with the object is the classic tell. Sits
              just under the bar's lowest edge at y≈604. {%- endcomment -%}
          <ellipse cx="365" cy="638" rx="288" ry="40" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-shadow)"/>
          <ellipse cx="368" cy="626" rx="212" ry="22" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-ao)"/>
        </g>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} ================= L1 · botanicals behind ============== {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="0.25"{% endif %}>
        <g class="sh-plate__layer">

          {%- comment -%} A small herb sprig, upper left. The quietest thing in the
              frame and deliberately so — it exists to stop the left edge going
              dead above the tulsi, nothing more.

              Leaflets, not bare ellipses. Two lone ellipses on a line read as
              pins or a dandelion clock, not foliage: a leaf needs a visible
              attachment angle to the stem before the eye files it as a plant. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g class="sh-plate__sway" style="--sh-plate-o:158px 436px; --sh-plate-d:-11s;">
            <path class="sh-plate__stem sh-plate__stem--far"
                  d="M158 436 C176 386 198 334 224 288"/>
            <g fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-b)">
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(174 392) rotate(-118) scale(0.8)"/>
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(174 392) rotate(-16)  scale(0.72)"/>
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(196 344) rotate(-116) scale(0.7)"/>
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(196 344) rotate(-18)  scale(0.62)"/>
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(224 288) rotate(-62)  scale(0.6)"/>
            </g>
          </g>

          {%- comment -%} Neem sprig, tucked behind the bar's top-right corner.
              Pinnate: a rachis with leaflets in opposite pairs, scaling down
              toward the tip, plus one terminal leaflet on the axis. The rachis
              runs up-right at about -56°, so leaflets sit at roughly -106° and
              -6° — near-vertical on the left, near-horizontal on the right. That
              asymmetry is real neem, not a mistake.

              The placement translate is on a WRAPPER, not on the animated group —
              rule (4). Putting it on .sh-plate__leaf-b would work until the
              keyframe in §25 overwrote it and the whole sprig jumped back to its
              authored coordinates. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g transform="translate(-10 150)">
            <g class="sh-plate__leaf-b" style="--sh-plate-o:556px 306px; --sh-plate-d:-4s;">
              <path class="sh-plate__stem sh-plate__stem--far"
                    d="M556 306 C588 268 618 218 646 170"/>
              <g fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-b)">
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(570 288) rotate(-110) scale(0.95)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(570 288) rotate(-3)   scale(0.95)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(584 269) rotate(-108) scale(0.89)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(584 269) rotate(-5)   scale(0.89)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(598 249) rotate(-106) scale(0.83)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(598 249) rotate(-7)   scale(0.83)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(611 228) rotate(-104) scale(0.77)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(611 228) rotate(-9)   scale(0.77)"/>
                <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" transform="translate(646 170) rotate(-57)  scale(0.72)"/>
              </g>
            </g>
          </g>

        </g>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} ================= L2 · the soap ======================= {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Depth 0.5 → ~4px of cursor travel, which is the 3–5px the
          brief asks for. The hero object moving least is the point: it is what
          the parallax is measured against. {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="0.5"{% endif %}>
        <g class="sh-plate__layer">
          <g class="sh-plate__float">
            {%- comment -%} One rotation for the whole solid, about the bar's own
                centre (375,517). An oblique projection with depth vector
                (-34,-104): back is up and slightly LEFT, so the RIGHT end is the
                one we see. Faces are painted far-to-near — end, front, top — so
                seams never need a stroke to hide a gap.

                The lean is 18° off vertical, not the 32° this started at. At 32°
                the top face overhung the front face by 52 units and the
                silhouette turned a hard corner at the front-left, which read as a
                notch in the solid — the bar looked like a doorstop. Depth also
                went from 82 units to 104: at 442 units for a 90mm bar the scale
                is 4.9 u/mm, so a 60mm depth seen from ~35° above wants roughly
                this, and the shallower version read as a tile. {%- endcomment -%}
            <g transform="rotate(-6.5 375 517)">

              <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-end)"
                    d="M596 540 C585 505 573 470 562 436 L562 492
                       C570 522 582 552 596 578 Z"/>

              <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-front)"
                    d="M188 540 C300 534 460 536 596 540 L596 578
                       C596 590 587 598 573 599 C450 604 300 603 208 599
                       C194 598 186 590 186 578 Z"/>

              <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-top)"
                    d="M188 540 C300 534 460 536 596 540 C585 505 573 470 562 436
                       C450 430 290 431 154 436 C165 470 177 505 188 540 Z"/>

              {%- comment -%} Bevel. Back and left edges ONLY — they are the two
                  turned toward the key. Running it the whole way round is what
                  makes a soap bar look like a sticker. {%- endcomment -%}
              <path class="sh-plate__bevel"
                    d="M188 540 C177 505 165 470 154 436 C290 431 450 430 562 436"/>

              {%- comment -%} The pressed wordmark. Read the matrix rather than
                  guessing at it: (1.42, 0) keeps x along the horizontal front
                  edge at the bar's scale, and (0.628, 0.9585) lays y down the
                  receding depth axis with its foreshortening already baked in.

                  Debossed, not embossed: soap branding is pressed IN, so with a
                  key from the upper left the shadow falls on the near-upper wall
                  and the highlight on the far-lower one. Inverting that pair is
                  the difference between "stamped" and "stuck on".

                  textLength is identical on all three copies and pinned with
                  lengthAdjust, so Cormorant arriving late re-cuts the glyphs
                  without ever letting the mark grow past the bar. Measured: the
                  mark occupies about 65% of the top face's width. {%- endcomment -%}
              <g class="sh-plate__mark">
                <text transform="matrix(1.42 0 0.311 0.951 373.6 486.4)" text-anchor="middle"
                      textLength="188" lengthAdjust="spacingAndGlyphs"
                      fill="#8a6a2c" fill-opacity="0.50">SHUDHI</text>
                <text transform="matrix(1.42 0 0.311 0.951 376.4 489.8)" text-anchor="middle"
                      textLength="188" lengthAdjust="spacingAndGlyphs"
                      fill="#f7ecd5" fill-opacity="0.55">SHUDHI</text>
                <text transform="matrix(1.42 0 0.311 0.951 375 488)" text-anchor="middle"
                      textLength="188" lengthAdjust="spacingAndGlyphs"
                      fill="#b09468" fill-opacity="0.75">SHUDHI</text>
              </g>

              {%- comment -%} A single bead left on the face. One, not a scatter:
                  it says the bar has been used, which is the whole difference
                  between a still life and a packshot.

                  Set back at x 544–568, in the empty quarter between the mark's
                  right edge (513) and the face's (596). It was at 483 and
                  overlapped the mark by 30 units, which made it read as a full
                  stop after "SHUDHI" rather than as water. {%- endcomment -%}
              <g class="sh-plate__bead">
                <ellipse cx="556" cy="456" rx="12.1" ry="8.5"
                         fill="url(#{{ gid }}-drop)" transform="rotate(-18 556 456)"/>
                <ellipse cx="552.6" cy="452.8" rx="3.6" ry="2.1"
                         fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity="0.75" transform="rotate(-18 553 453)"/>
              </g>

              {%- comment -%} A parallelogram, not a skewed <rect>. skewX shears
                  as a function of y, so a rect this far down the viewBox was
                  translated hundreds of units sideways as a side effect and blew
                  the soap group's bbox out to 1090 units — which is the box a
                  promoted compositor layer gets sized to. The lean is baked into
                  the path instead: 210 units of height against 34/104 of run,
                  matching the depth vector.

                  The geometry sits ON the face (x 154–343), not parked off to the
                  left, so getBBox stays inside the bar and the promoted layer
                  stays small. Authoring it off-frame instead cost 270 units of
                  empty bbox for nothing.

                  It is invisible at rest because §25 gives it opacity:0, and the
                  keyframe ramps opacity as well as position. That satisfies (3)
                  without the off-frame parking: with no animation running — still
                  mode, reduced motion, JS absent — there is no bright band on the
                  soap, because there is no opacity. Nothing is hidden that a
                  reader needed; the sheen is the one purely decorative mark in
                  the piece. {%- endcomment -%}
              <g clip-path="url(#{{ gid }}-topclip)">
                <path class="sh-plate__sheen" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-sheen)"
                      d="M154 410 L274 410 L343 620 L223 620 Z"/>
              </g>

            </g>
          </g>
        </g>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} ================= L3 · mid ============================ {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="0.75"{% endif %}>
        <g class="sh-plate__layer">

          {%- comment -%} Bud, off-centre left. Sits in what would otherwise be
              the frame's one genuinely dead region — the void above the bar and
              between the bare stem and the neem. Off-centre because dead centre
              would split the top of the composition in half. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g class="sh-plate__leaf-b" style="--sh-plate-o:330px 366px; --sh-plate-d:-9s;">
            <path class="sh-plate__stem" d="M330 366 C333 344 336 322 334 300"/>
            {%- comment -%} A closed bud: two overlapping sepals with a lighter
                one in front, then the gold tip. Drawn larger than a real one at
                this scale — at the previous size it was a 30-unit sliver reading
                as a stray mark rather than a form. {%- endcomment -%}
            <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-a)"
                  d="M334 300 C322 286 322 264 334 248 C346 264 346 286 334 300 Z"/>
            <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-b)" fill-opacity="0.9"
                  d="M334 300 C328 288 328 268 334 254 C340 268 340 288 334 300 Z"/>
            <ellipse cx="334" cy="245" rx="4" ry="4.6" fill="#c9a86a" fill-opacity="0.78"/>
            <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-a)"
                 transform="translate(332 330) rotate(-152) scale(0.66)"/>
            <use href="#{{ gid }}-leaflet" fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-a)"
                 transform="translate(333 344) rotate(-24) scale(0.6)"/>
          </g>

          {%- comment -%} A free droplet catching the key, mid-air, right of the
              bar. Balances the bead ON the bar without repeating it, and holds
              the right edge between the neem above and the herb below. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-14s;">
            <path fill="url(#{{ gid }}-drop)"
                  d="M660 486 C669 498 674 507 674 514 C674 522 668 528 660 528
                     C652 528 646 522 646 514 C646 507 651 498 660 486 Z"/>
            <ellipse cx="656" cy="515" rx="3" ry="4" fill="#ffffff" fill-opacity="0.62"/>
          </g>

        </g>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} ================= L4 · foreground ===================== {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Depth 1.0 → the full 8px. Nearest the viewer, so it moves
          most and is painted in the saturated green. Both clusters sit LOW —
          bottom-left and bottom-right — which is what stops the composition
          reading as a wreath around the soap. {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="1"{% endif %}>
        <g class="sh-plate__layer">

          {%- comment -%} Tulsi, lower left. Opposite leaves on one stem, the near
              one larger. Two things are placed rather than drawn here:

              The cluster is held at x ≥ 208. Everything left of that is the
              seal's corner — see (7). The clearance is about 94 units and it is
              the only reason the seal reads as a signature on the composition
              rather than a sticker over it. Moving the tulsi left closes it.

              The topmost leaf deliberately crosses the bar's lower-left corner at
              (193,602). Foreground overlapping the hero is what makes the depth
              read at all when parallax is off — which is most of the time, on
              touch and under reduced motion. It clears the wordmark by ~120
              units.

              The three loops are -a, -b, -a with co-prime periods and unrelated
              negative delays: a matched pair breathing in sync reads mechanical. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-a)">
            <path class="sh-plate__stem" d="M274 806 C256 760 240 704 232 648"/>

            <g class="sh-plate__leaf-a" style="--sh-plate-o:232px 648px; --sh-plate-d:-2s;">
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-tulsi" transform="translate(232 648) rotate(-34) scale(1.05)"/>
            </g>
            <g class="sh-plate__leaf-b" style="--sh-plate-o:256px 716px; --sh-plate-d:-7.5s;">
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-tulsi" transform="translate(256 716) rotate(158) scale(0.92)"/>
            </g>
            <g class="sh-plate__leaf-a" style="--sh-plate-o:246px 690px; --sh-plate-d:-12s;">
              <use href="#{{ gid }}-tulsi" transform="translate(246 690) rotate(-16) scale(0.7)"/>
            </g>
          </g>

          {%- comment -%} Low herb cluster, bottom right. Three blades from one
              base — the counterweight to the tulsi, deliberately smaller and set
              higher so the two do not read as a symmetric pair. {%- endcomment -%}
          <g class="sh-plate__sway" style="--sh-plate-o:604px 760px; --sh-plate-d:-5.5s;"
             fill="url(#{{ gid }}-leaf-a)">
            <path d="M604 760 C596 726 600 696 616 670 C624 702 620 734 604 760 Z"/>
            <path d="M604 760 C582 738 570 714 570 688 C592 704 604 732 604 760 Z"
                  fill-opacity="0.82"/>
            <path d="M604 760 C620 742 640 730 662 728 C648 746 626 758 604 760 Z"
                  fill-opacity="0.7"/>
          </g>

        </g>
      </g>

      {%- comment -%} ================= L5 · particles ====================== {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Depth 0.4, not 1.0 — the brief asks that particles react
          minimally, and dust that tracks the cursor hard stops reading as dust.
          Seven, scattered off-grid, none of them landing on the bar, all inside
          the y-band in (6). {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-plate__depth"{% if cursor %} data-plate-depth="0.4"{% endif %}>
        {%- comment -%} Each mote states its opacity TWICE, and both are needed.
            The presentation attribute is the resting value — what shows with no
            animation at all, which is the still case. --sh-plate-op is the same
            number where the keyframe can reach it, so the pulse scales each mote
            against its own value instead of driving them all to a common peak
            and flattening the scatter into a row of matching dots. {%- endcomment -%}
        <g class="sh-plate__layer sh-plate__motes" fill="#a98d5c">
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-3s;    --sh-plate-op:.22" cx="306" cy="322" r="2.4" opacity="0.22"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-17s;   --sh-plate-op:.18" cx="470" cy="250" r="1.8" opacity="0.18"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-8.5s;  --sh-plate-op:.20" cx="152" cy="372" r="3"   opacity="0.20"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-21s;   --sh-plate-op:.26" cx="688" cy="618" r="2.2" opacity="0.26"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-12.5s; --sh-plate-op:.16" cx="392" cy="208" r="1.6" opacity="0.16"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-6s;    --sh-plate-op:.19" cx="546" cy="706" r="2.6" opacity="0.19"/>
          <circle class="sh-plate__drift" style="--sh-plate-d:-19s;   --sh-plate-op:.24" cx="252" cy="790" r="2"   opacity="0.24"/>
        </g>
      </g>

    </g>
  </svg>
